Z||000022||WKDFIRO||04-27-2001||01:52 PM||||Speaking of gas tank locations, having the tank in the back or down the center of the car under your right arm (or left for our Aussie counterparts) is not nearly as unnerving as in the 1930 Model A. <P>The gas tank had to use gravity to flow down to the carb making its location convenient. It was the DASHBOARD. Reach past the steering wheel tap on the mechanical speedometer and know that the fuel is right there!<P>As for kit cars that are built better than the real thing. Im glad someone has seen them. The kit cars I have seen have been fiberglass abominations from the word Go. Its sad that a few good kit cars I see at the shows (Knotts Kit Car show tomorrow)are not the ones I have seen come into the shop with uneven fiberglass, high school welding jobs and electrical nightmares.||||reg||12
Z||000023||Jay||04-29-2001||05:46 PM||||Gabe, why not look at the real car?  The Ferrari Dino 246, 308, 328 and 348 all had gas tanks just infront of the rear wheels.  In fact they were two tanks, one on the left and one on the right.  I'm not sure about the 512 TR, I haven't looked at one in a few years.  They could do this because as you may know, the Ferraris have a bigger interior compartment.  There is actually room behind the seats. Check out some of the Ferrari forums.  I don't  think that it is a crazy idea.  I've got drawings of the fuel tanks in Ferraris if you are interested.  E-mail me and I'll scan them and send them to you. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Jay<BR>1986 Northstar GT <A HREF="http://www.members.home.net/jaygrande" TARGET=_blank>www.members.home.net/jaygrande</A> ||||reg||1
